  "batch-baked-potatoes": {
    "title": "A Tray of Five Baked Potatoes",
    "description": "Bake five at once on a good day, then reheat one at a time with whatever topping the week calls for. The most reliable no-thought dinner there is.",
    "origin": "American",
    "time": "1 hr (then 10 min per dinner)",
    "servings": 5,
    "instant_pot": false,
    "section": "Dinner",
    "tags": [
      "Dinner",
      "Assembly",
      "Make Ahead",
      "Vegetarian"
    ],
    "ingredients": [
      {
        "amount": 5,
        "unit": null,
        "name": "russet potatoes, medium to large",
        "location": "Counter",
        "category": "Produce"
      },
      {
        "amount": 2,
        "unit": "tbsp",
        "name": "olive oil",
        "location": "Pantry",
        "category": "Oils & Vinegars"
      },
      {
        "amount": 2,
        "unit": "tsp",
        "name": "coarse salt",
        "location": "Spice Rack",
        "category": "Spices"
      }
    ],
    "steps": [
      {
        "title": "Prep the tray",
        "text": "Heat the oven to 425°F. Scrub the potatoes dry and prick each one four or five times with a fork — this lets steam escape and keeps them from splitting. Rub each with olive oil and roll in the coarse salt."
      },
      {
        "title": "Bake",
        "text": "Set them directly on a rimmed sheet pan, not touching, and bake 50–60 minutes. They're done when a knife slides into the center with no resistance and the skins feel papery and crisp. No foil — foil steams the skin soft, and the crisp skin is half the point."
      },
      {
        "title": "Cool and store",
        "text": "Let them cool completely on the counter, then refrigerate within two hours in a container, unwrapped or loosely covered. Don't store them wrapped in foil and don't leave them out overnight — baked potatoes held warm in foil are one of the few genuine food safety risks in a home kitchen."
      },
      {
        "title": "Reheat one at a time",
        "text": "From the fridge: 400°F oven for 15 minutes brings the skin back. Microwave for 2–3 minutes is faster and perfectly fine — the skin goes soft, that's the trade. Split it open, fluff the inside with a fork, and top."
      }
    ],
    "notes": "Five topping combinations that need no cooking beyond what's already in your fridge: black beans, salsa, avocado, and toasted pepitas; muhammara with crumbled feta and parsley; leftover pumpkin chili with yogurt and pickled jalapeños; tahini dressing, chickpeas, and wilted greens; a fried egg with olive oil and flaky salt. Sweet potatoes work identically but need 45–50 minutes. Keeps 5 days refrigerated.\nA reheated potato becomes a proper dinner with almost no effort: a candle, some music, a few flowers in a bud vase, and fresh herbs or something from the garden scattered over the top. The plate is the same. The meal isn't."
  },
